The lab report should be in a single .tex file. The header should include the date, your name, and your TA’s name. Your TA will distribute a template. The lab report should include all the code you wrote for this assignment. You can upload it as a single .zip file.

#### Instructions

• Lab report in .tex

#### Submission

• Assignments must be submitted on Castor before 11:59 PM of Friday, June 4, 2021. Assignments submitted after the deadline are considered late. Late assignments will receive a 10% penalty for each day late.
• You must submit as a .zip file and .tex file. For example, if the name of your code is report.tex, you must submit a .zip file called “report.zip” that contains report.tex. If your code is not named “report.tex”, you must submit the .zip file with the same name as the .tex file. For example, if your code is named “report2.tex” you must submit a .zip file named “report2.zip” that contains report2.tex.
• You must submit your code as a .tex file. Do not submit .pdf or .jpg files. Any text-related files you submit that are not .tex files will not be graded.
• If you are using TeXstudio, you can compile a .tex file into a .pdf file by clicking the button with the green check mark and a red arrow. If you are using Overleaf, you can compile a .tex file into a .pdf file by clicking the button that looks like a bug.
• If you are not able to submit your assignment on Castor because of technical problems, you must send a support ticket to the support desk. Do not send an email to the instructors.

#### Grading

• The main goal of this assignment is to demonstrate that you can write Fortran 90. We will pass your Fortran 90 code through a Fortran 90 linter and check to make sure it’s valid code. This means that we will only spend 5% of the grade on correctness. The remaining 95% of the grade is determined by the style of your code. You are not required to use proper style to pass the linter, but you will not receive a good grade if you do not style your code well. You should follow the style guidelines below.
• Please use the following guidelines to style your code:
• Use proper case for variables (e.g., temperature instead of temperature)
• Don’t use underscores in your variables (e.g., temperature instead of temperature)
• Don’t capitalize keywords (e.g., DO instead of Do)
• Use indentation (e.g., if (convection) then .... elseif (diffusion) then ... end if)
• You should not get any warnings when you run the validator. If you do, you should fix all of the warnings. Please do not try to get around the validator by explicitly assigning the value of a variable or by adding extra empty lines. If you do, you will get a zero for that assignment. We do understand that the validator will sometimes return erroneous warnings. Please make sure that you fix all of the warnings you get, and if you are unsure, you can ask your TA.

#### Completion

For this assignment, we will determine the completion rate of the assignment based on the number of points you earn relative to the maximum number of points you could earn. In other words, the completion rate will equal 100% if you get a full score on the assignment. In contrast, the completion rate will equal 0% if you get a score of 0 on the assignment. Specifically, the completion rate is defined to be 100% x (max score - score) / (max score - min score), where score is the number of points the student earned, and min score is the lowest possible number of points that a student could earn on the assignment.

#### Late Policy

If you meet any of the following conditions, you may request an extension before the due date and time for 25% of the total points of the assignment. If you submit your assignment late without requesting an extension first, you will not be eligible for an extension.
• You are affected by an emergency (e.g., death in the family, serious illness, natural disaster, etc.)
• You were in direct contact with someone with COVID-19
