cutable file with the .e extension. Freund, William 10 18 1998 4/25/00 3:17 PM Page 37 Figure 9.3: The process of compiling, assembling, and linking a C program when it is input as a string. Freund, William Freund, William 10 18 1998 4/25/00 3:17 PM Page 38 Freund, William Freund, William 10 18 1998 4/25/00 3:17 PM Page 38 343 Freund, William Summary Functions and procedures can be used in C programs to make the programs easier to write and understand. Functions and procedures are similar in terms of the input data and the output data. However, procedures are input- and output-free. A function can be used to compute a value and return it as the value of the function. A procedure can be used to perform a task that does not produce a value. Freund, William 10 18 1998 4/25/00 3:17 PM Page 39 Freund, William Freund, William 10 18 1998 4/25/00 3:17 PM Page 39 344 Freund, William Exercises 1. Consider the following function. int x(int i) { int m = 3 * i * (i + 10) / 4; return m; } (a) Write a short C program that uses this function. (b) Write an English description of the function. 2. Consider the function foo that is defined in the following program. int main(void) { int x = 0; while (x < 10) { if (foo(x) < 0) { x = x + 1; } else { x = x + 2; } } return 0; } int foo(int x) { int m = 3 * x; if (m > 10) { m = m - 10; } else { m = m + 10; } return m; } (a) Write a short English description of the program. (b) Execute the program", "indてlistitem|uirichText|uirichBox|uiFw|uiFw|uiFw|uiFw|uirichBox|uirichText|uiBox|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichText|uiBox|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichText|uirichText|uirichText|uirichText|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|uirichBox|